{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion bias: The model consistently forecasts a slow, linear drift based on recent history, failing to anticipate sharp regime shifts (e.g., the ~15 EUR spike in late Jan 2023). Apply a step-function adjustment when the 3-day price change exceeds 3%.",
    "Invert sentiment weighting: Positive sentiment spikes (values > 0.20) in the history correlate with immediate price drops in the short term (lag 1-3 days) before the actual rally, suggesting the model overreacts to sentiment as a leading indicator rather than a lagging confirmation.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon slope: The model's 30-day projection exhibits an artificial upward drift (approx. 0.04 EUR/day) that ignores volatility clustering. Flatten the forecast slope by 50% and introduce a mean-reversion term pulling the forecast toward the 20-day moving average.",
    "Account for 'overshoot' lag: When true prices spike (e.g., >90 EUR), the model lags by 5-7 days before adjusting. Implement a momentum correction factor that accelerates the forecast slope only after a confirmed 3-day consecutive rise above the 20-day MA.",
    "Ignore low-magnitude sentiment noise: Sentiment values between 0.00 and 0.25 show no predictive power for the model's linear trend and should be treated as noise; only sentiment > 0.30 should trigger a volatility expansion in the forecast error bands."
  ]
}
